<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for xenra-ios.

  This file is an INPUT to the codesign entitlements flag, not something that
  copied into the bundle. Entitlements exist only inside a code signature; a
  plist sitting in a .app grants nothing. The build is deliberately unsigned
  (see XenDroid/cmake/ios.toolchain.cmake) and this is applied either by the CI
  signing step when secrets are present, or by whatever tool the user signs
  with on their own machine.

  ============================================================================
  DO NOT COPY <XEN>/xenia.entitlements. IT IS A macOS FILE.
  ============================================================================
  Every key in it is prefixed com.apple.security.cs.* - the "cs" is Code
  Signing / Hardened Runtime, which is a macOS-only mechanism. In particular
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit does NOTHING on iOS. It is not "ignored but
  harmless as documentation": iOS has no Hardened Runtime, MAP_JIT on iOS is
  gated on the process being debuggable rather than on an entitlement, and
  believing that key is what turns JIT on will send you looking for the
  problem in entirely the wrong place. What actually gates JIT on iOS is
  com.apple.security.get-task-allow plus a debugger having attached at least
  once - see XenDroid/docs/ios-build.md, which is the only place that story is
  written down correctly.

  get-task-allow is NOT listed here. It is not ours to grant: it comes from
  the provisioning profile / signing flow (a development-signed build has it;
  an ad-hoc or distribution-signed build does not), and listing it in a file
  that a distribution signature then strips only creates a false sense that it
  is handled.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Raises the app's jetsam (memory) limit from the default per-device cap
	  toward the physical RAM ceiling. A 360 title's 512 MB guest RAM plus the
	  JIT code cache plus host texture staging goes past the default limit on
	  every device we care about, and exceeding it is an instant kill with no
	  catchable signal.

	  REQUIRES APPLE APPROVAL. This is a "restricted" entitlement: it only
	  takes effect if it is present in a provisioning profile Apple issued for
	  a team that requested and was granted it. On a free-account or
	  self-signed build it is silently ignored - installation still succeeds,
	  the app just gets the ordinary limit. So do not read a successful
	  install as evidence that it is active; measure the limit at runtime.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Raises the jetsam limit further while a debugger is attached. Xenra's
	  JIT already needs one attached (StikDebug or Xcode), so on those
	  launches this may lift the cap past increased-memory-limit. XeniOS has
	  shipped it in xenia_ios.entitlements since its first port (16d9272f8).
	  Whether it raises the limit on a sideloaded build is unmeasured: read
	  the limit the log reports at launch before crediting it.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-debugging-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Enables the 64-bit (rather than 36-bit) virtual address space for the
	  process. The guest address space mapping in base/memory.cc reserves a
	  large contiguous region and lets the OS choose the base (MapViewsMac);
	  without extended addressing that reservation competes with everything
	  else in a cramped VA space and fails intermittently, which presents as a
	  startup crash on some devices and not others.

	  Same caveat as above: restricted, requires Apple approval on the profile,
	  silently inert without it.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Read/write access to files the user picks through the document picker.
	  Not needed by milestone 1, which loads nothing, but it is how a game will
	  ever get in: there is no general filesystem on iOS, so a title has to be
	  handed over explicitly.

	  Taken from XeniOS (github.com/xenios-jp/XeniOS, BSD-3-Clause, same licence
	  as this tree). XeniOS's iOS entitlements (as of cef52a33f) now also carry
	  extended-virtual-addressing (2da5b4d5c), increased-memory-limit,
	  increased-debugging-memory-limit and get-task-allow; an earlier note here
	  saying they did not ship extended-virtual-addressing is out of date.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
